<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mangkunegara I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Raden Mas Said, Pangeran Sambernyawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mangkunegara I, born Raden Mas Said and known as Pangeran Sambernyawa, was a Javanese prince of the house of Mataram who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the royal courts for about sixteen years. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> in 1757 he received an apanage in Surakarta and founded the princely house of Mangkunegaran. He is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i.jpg" alt="Mangkunegara I" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uncredited, published by Direktorat K2KRS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mangkunagara_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raden Mas Said was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, the son of Raden Ayu Wulan and Prince Mangkunegara of Kartasura, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iv">Amangkurat IV</a> by a concubine and crown prince of Mataram. His mother was a daughter of Prince Blitar, Amangkurat IV's younger brother, so his parents were first cousins. His name Sambernyawa, the life-reaping prince, came from his banner of the same name. He married Raden Ayu Kusumapatahati, daughter of the cleric Kyai Kasan Nur Iman, and later Raden Ajeng Inten, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Prince Mangkubumi</a>, afterwards Hamengkubuwana I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His war began with the rebellion at Kartasura Palace on 30 June 1742, led by Raden Mas Garendi, also called Sunan Kuning, which drove King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwono II</a> to Ponorogo. Said joined his uncle Mangkubumi against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the king, whom they saw as its puppet, built a fortress at Randuwalang north of Surakarta and joined his men to Sunan Kuning's forces. Pakubuwono II paid for the Company's help by ceding the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from Rembang to Surabaya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and by submitting court appointments to its approval.</p><p>Said later separated from Garendi, set up headquarters at Panambangan and declared himself Prince Mangkunegara I. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwono II</a>'s death he urged Mangkubumi to proclaim himself king, and Mangkubumi became Sultan Hamengkubuwono, with Said as his warlord. The two then fell out, and Said fought the Company alone. He rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 13 February 1755, which divided Mataram, as a blow to the unity of its people. Over 16 years he fought 250 battles, and even the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor Baron van Hohendorff admitted his skill in war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> in 1757 his alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Hamengkubuwana I</a> turned to rivalry. It deepened when Gusti Kanjeng Ratu Alit, eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a>, married Said's eldest son rather than the crown prince of Yogyakarta, a slight that led Said's wife, Ratu Bendara, to return to Yogyakarta and seek a divorce. Said died on 28 December 1795. The son of that marriage, Pangeran Arya Prangwadana, succeeded his grandfather as Mangkunegara II, and the Babad Panambangan of 1918 lists more than twenty of Mangkunegara I's children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mangkunegara I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Raden Mas Said</b>, a Mataram prince known as Pangeran Sambernyawa, fought both the Dutch East India Company and the royal courts for years.</blockquote><a href="/state/mangkunegaran">Mangkunegaran</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Surakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.76&amp;year=1770" aria-label="Kartasura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mangkunegara I"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4220 5645h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4220 5645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.57,110.76&amp;year=1770">What was here in 1770? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
